      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "First, we consider the multiples of the integer. The multiples of \\(1000\\) can be expressed as \\(1000k\\) where \\(k\\) is an integer. We need to find the multiples of \\(1000\\) whose quotient is an integer when \\(1000\\) is divided by the chosen integer.\n\nFor any integer \\(m\\) such that \\(1 \\leq m \\leq 1000\\), \\(m\\) must be evenly divisible by \\(1000\\). That is, when \\(1000\\) is divided by \\(m\\), the quotient is an integer. For \\(m\\) to be a proper divisor of \\(1000\\), \\(m\\) must also be a divisor of \\(1000\\). \n\nThe divisors of \\(1000\\) are \\(1, 2, 4, 5, 8, 10, 20, 25, 40, 50, 100, 125, 200, 250, 500,\\) and \\(1000\\).\n\nThus, for each \\(m\\) that is a divisor of \\(1000\\), \\(m / 1000\\) is an integer. This means there are 13 divisors of \\(1000\\) from \\(1\\) to \\(1000\\).\n\nTherefore, the number of integers Josef could pick such that Timothy's quotient is an integer is \\(\\boxed{13}\\).",
