    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "The outcome is determined by the prime factorization of 1000. By definition, the quotient is an integer when the prime factors of the chosen integer cancel out all the prime factors of 1000.\n\nHowever, 1000 already has a factor of 2^3, which makes it impossible for any integer to cancel out all the prime factors of 1000 and yield an integer. Thus, there are no such integers Josef could choose such that Timothy's quotient is an integer.\n\nHence, 0 integers.\n\nSo the final answer is $\\boxed{0}$.\n\nTo solve this problem, I confused the quotient and dividend, so I overlooked that 1000 only has two distinct prime factors, namely 2 and 5. It's easy to see that no powers of 2 or 5 can divide any number from 1 to 1000. However, the highest power of 5 that does divide up to 1000 is 25. So 1000 only has a chance to cancel 25. Since 25 is the largest power of a single prime number that can divide up to 1000, so it will cancel 2^3 and doesn't have any other prime powers to cancel. Thus, we are left with only 1 finite answer, which is 0.",
